      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine the area of the figure \\(ABCD\\), we need to break it down into simpler geometric shapes. The figure \\(ABCD\\) consists of two sectors of a circle. Let's analyze each sector step by step.\n\n1. **Identify the radius and central angle of each sector:**\n   Each sector is configured with a radius of 12 and a central angle of \\(60^\\circ\\). A \\(60^\\circ\\) angle corresponds to one-third of \\(\\pi\\) radians. Therefore, each sector has a central angle of \\(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\).\n\n2. **Calculate the area of one sector:**\n   The area \\(A\\) of a sector of a circle with radius \\(r\\) and central angle \\(\\theta\\) (in radians) is given by:\n   \\[\n   A = \\frac{1}{2} r^2 \\theta\n   \\]\n   Here, \\(r = 12\\) and \\(\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\). Substituting these values in, we get:\n   \\[\n   A = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 12^2 \\times \\frac{\\pi}{3} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 144 \\times \\frac{\\pi}{3} = 24\\pi\n   \\]\n   So, the area of one sector is \\(24\\pi\\).\n\n3. **Calculate the total area of the figure \\(ABCD\\):**\n   Since there are two such sectors, the total area \\(A_{\\text{total}}\\) of the figure \\(ABCD\\) is:\n   \\[\n   A_{\\text{total}} = 2 \\times 24\\pi = 48\\pi\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the area of the figure \\(ABCD\\) is \\(\\boxed{48\\pi}\\).",
